WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.049
Constanza Romero: I’m Constanza Romero and
I’m here representing my late husband August

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:05.049
Wilson.

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:12.400
He did the film adaptation for FENCES and
he also wrote the original play which played

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:21.210
on Broadway in 1984, 1985 and in 2010 with
Denzel Washington.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:27.840
Film Courage: Tell us about what you saw in
his (August’s) work ethic and how he would

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.070
dive into a story.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.910
How did he create a world on the page?

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.640
Constanza Romero: Well his work ethic was
this: to live was to work.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.380
And to work was to live.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.550
He was always working.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.430
He was always developing his characters.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:50.210
If we say down to eat he, was talking about
his work and his characters were like almost

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.570
real, you know?

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.890
He would tell me what a character was doing
and I was like “Oh, my God.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.940
He robbed a bank?”

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:10.400
So how he developed it, he wrote in long hand
and then he transferred it to the computer

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.940
and then he would always talk in his rhythm.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:23.630
Until it sounded good in his ear he wouldn’t
finalize it yet because he was all about…his

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:30.390
poetry was his music and if it didn’t sound
right to his ear, he rewrote it and rewrote

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.370
it.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.260
And that is where you can see where he was
a poet first.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.200
Film Courage: What can you share with our
viewers about being so close to someone that

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:47.290
is so involved in the creative process and
in some sense you are sharing your life with

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.490
another entity and that is their work?

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:58.640
Constanza Romero: Well, I’m a costume designer
so we collaborated on a lot of projects.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:05.140
I would be sort of his visual dramaturge so
to speak because I would find different photos

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:10.200
that were inspirational to him while they
were inspirational to me too because I was

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:18.730
doing the costumes and he would also say the
lines out loud and I would read his first

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.870
scripts and I would say “Well, what about
this character?

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.530
Could she be this or could she be that?”

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.580
And so the collaboration process was really
incredible.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.600
But sometimes it was a little too much.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:42.709
As I said, we would be downstairs eating and
he would go on, and on, and on, and on, about

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:44.120
the play and I would just say “Oh!

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.870
I’ve got to go to work.”

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:51.959
And he’d follow me up the stairs and eat
a sandwich right by my drawing table….but

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:54.980
it’s all just good memories.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:00.040
Film Courage: Any of the characters or one
specific character that was most like him

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.040
in some sense?

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.910
Constanza Romero: Oh….you know…I have
to say that all of the characters are like

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:15.709
him and although the character of Rose is
definitely inspired by his mother.

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:23.920
But I think that this is the amazing breadth
of his work is that all his characters had

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:34.480
little part of himself but they were also
so imaginatively created.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.720
So I can’t say that there is one person.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:45.840
For example when he was young, he was the
older son whose art nobody understood, you

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:46.840
know?

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.760
Because he was writing poetry on napkins and
menus.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:04:07.250
So the older son Lyons [in FENCES] he was
sort of like him carrying around his pen and

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:13.510
paper like he carries around his instrument
and playing at night but nobody in his family

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.900
understanding why the heck they don’t get
a job.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:18.900
Right!

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:19.900
Yes!

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.180
Film Courage: And lastly, what can you tell
others about what it’s like to share your

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.530
life with someone who at some points maybe
they are critical of their own work?

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.449
And watching them go through days that maybe
they didn’t feel good about their projects

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:36.690
even though others related to them but it
was such a tough process because they were

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.509
so close to the material and couldn’t see
it for what it was?

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:41.990
Constanza Romero: Yeah…August hated to cut.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:49.039
If somebody said the play is longer than three
hours, what are we going to do?

00:04:49.039 --> 00:04:56.620
So I think that he would be so stubborn, nobody
could make him cut something.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.309
And then finally one of the producers set
me aside and said “Can you tell him that

00:05:01.309 --> 00:05:03.439
Seven Guitars is a little too long.”

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:09.529
And actually I sat down and I could really
see where it could be snipped here and there

00:05:09.529 --> 00:05:12.240
and I was able to help him do that.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.219
But I believe I was one of the only people
he really trusted to tell him the truth as

00:05:18.219 --> 00:05:19.219
you’re saying.

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:30.279
And not every day was inspired, not every
day was a joy.

00:05:30.279 --> 00:05:33.380
Creating something is not always a joyful
process.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:41.559
It has a lot of raw emotions and you are practically
weaving gold.

00:05:41.559 --> 00:05:47.490
But the raw material is not always the gold
that it becomes later.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:48.490
Film Courage: Beautiful!

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:48.492
Thank you!

